The goal of this study is to recognize and address bias in solar image sequences that feature vector magnetic field data inference. To achieve this, we set up a neural network that predicts and assigns a bias score to each sequence. The network is trained on the SLORv3 inference method's output for specific solar phenomena. We also develop and train a bias correction transformer, which is combined with SLORv3 and validated using \emph{Helioseismic and Magnetic InstrumenT} data. Through comparison with other inference techniques, we demonstrate that the bias corrected \emph{Magnetic Instumentation} data has reduced discrepancies with actual \emph{Helioseismic and Magnetic InstumentT} data. Moreover, we visualize the impact of bias in sunspot regions and highlight the importance of accounting for it in solar image processing. The developed transformer is designed to be adaptable for use with other vector magnetic field inference techniques. Therefore, this research provides a foundation for applying bias correction across the community. The code is available at \href{https://github.com/AndreiCiprian/bias-transformer}{this repository}. 

